Send multi-line prompts to Claude Code as a bracketed paste
Closes #179.

Two commits, deliberately separate so you can judge them independently.

## 1 — Revert the `WM_NULL` paste handoff from #178

#178 assumed the Enter could overtake the paste in conhost's queue. It cannot: `ID_CONSOLE_PASTE` and the Enter `WM_CHAR` are posted to the same window, and posted messages are delivered in order. The handoff never changed the outcome, and its comment describes a failure that cannot happen. This commit restores `PasteViaConhostPasteCommandAsync` byte-for-byte to its state before #178. My mistake — sorry for the churn.

## 2 — Send multi-line prompts to Claude Code as a bracketed paste

`WrapMultilineAsBracketedPaste` (next to `StripTrailingNewlines`, same `internal static` style) wraps text containing a line break in `ESC[200~` … `ESC[201~`, normalizes line breaks to LF and drops any ESC from the body. `SendTextToTerminalAsync` applies it right after `StripTrailingNewlines`, **only for Claude Code in conhost**:

```csharp
if (_wtTabBarHeight == 0 && _currentRunningProvider == AiProvider.ClaudeCode)
{
    text = WrapMultilineAsBracketedPaste(text);
}
```

Everything after that is unchanged: the same clipboard paste, the same wait, the same separate Enter.

### Why

conhost's Edit→Paste turns every line break into an Enter and sends no paste markers, so Claude Code guesses from timing whether it is being typed at or pasted into (the "paste-burst window" from the #108 remark). A short multi-line prompt is taken as typing and split into several messages at its line breaks; a longer one is taken as a paste, and because its line breaks arrive as lone CRs — which Claude Code's hidden-character check counts — it is held back with "review and press Enter to send". Details and measurements are in #179.

The markers remove the guess. It is the form Claude Code uses itself when it writes into another session's terminal — text wrapped in `\x1B[200~` / `\x1B[201~`, then `"\r"` written separately.

### Scope, on purpose

- **Claude Code only.** The other agents are not known to handle the markers, and for a TUI that does not, the leading ESC could even act as Escape and interrupt it.
- **conhost only.** Windows Terminal wraps pastes itself when the app asks for bracketed paste; adding our own markers there would double them.
- **Multi-line only.** Single-line prompts and slash commands (`/model`, `/effort`, `/compact`, …) keep exactly the plain paste they always used.
- `ClaudeCodeWSL` is left out because I could not test it; it goes through the same conhost paste, so it very likely benefits too, if you want to include it.

## Verification

- `ClaudeCodeExtension.sln`, Release — 0 errors, no new warnings, VSIX produced.
- Unit tests: **491 passed, 0 failed**, including three new ones in `TerminalPayloadTests`: CRLF / LF / lone CR normalized and wrapped, single-line and empty text untouched, and an ESC in the text cannot close the paste early.
